# tools/ales_stereo_shelf_witness.rish — stereo_shelf: the two-band bass-and-treble tone shelf carried into stereo, the same coefficient and band gains on both channels — the second rung of the stereo EQ / filter class (ALES171).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES171.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-075512_fill-ales171-lotus-stereo-shelf.md
# Stands on: lotus/shelf.rye (ALES41 — the mono two-band shelf run once per channel, its ShelfError reused whole), lotus/tone.rye (ALES40 — the low-pass/high-pass split the shelf scales), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the
# StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate the shelf's sum reuses).
#
# lotus/stereo_shelf.rye shapes a stereo master's bass and treble: it runs ALES41's proven mono two-band shelf on BOTH channels with the SAME coefficient and band gains — split each channel's span into a low band and its exact
# high band, scale each by its own gain, and write the sum saturated once. A shelf's knobs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image
# for free; each channel shelves its own content, and unlike the tone control there is no carried state to thread (the shelf runs a fresh low-pass over the whole span each call). It validates the coefficient, both band gains,
# and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadCoeff on an illegal filter coefficient, BadGain on a zero band denominator, BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never shelves
# one channel and leaves the other torn; ShelfError reused whole. Values only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is four laws: the stereo shelf law (each channel equal to ALES41's mono shelf with the
# same knobs byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the flat-EQ identity law (unity on both bands the identity on both, the bands reconstructing the input, bass moving direct current while treble leaves it untouched
# per channel), the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the shelf, the shared knobs holding the image), and the balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_shelf_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-shelf: Language EN — stereo_shelf shapes a stereo master's bass and treble by running ALES41's proven mono two-band shelf on both channels with the same coefficient and band gains: split each channel's span into a low band and its exact high band, scale each by its own gain, and write the sum saturated once. A shelf's knobs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image for free; each channel shelves its own content, and unlike the tone control there is no carried state to thread. It validates the coefficient, both band gains, and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never shelves one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-shelf: Style Radiant — ALES170 opened the stereo EQ and filter class with the one-pole tone control, yet a filter alone only removes a band; the tone control a keeper actually turns is two knobs, bass and treble, each boosting or cutting its band independently. ALES41 built that for a mono clip: split the span into a low band and its exact high band, scale each by its own gain, and write the sum saturated once over the true wide-domain total. This rung carries it into stereo the way the EQ class asks — run ALES41's proven shelf on each channel with the same coefficient and band gains, so bass and treble turn on both speakers together. Like every parametric edit, a shelf's coefficient and band gains are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image for free, with no linking and no measurement; each channel shelves its own content, which is exactly right for equalization — an identical-channel, mono-in-stereo master stays identical because the same input through the same knobs is the same deterministic output, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum. Unlike the tone control, there is no carried state to lift: the shelf runs a fresh low-pass over the whole span each call, so a stereo shelf is a stateless per-channel pass with nothing to thread across a seam. The crux is four laws: the stereo shelf law (each channel equal to ALES41's mono shelf with the same coefficient and band gains byte for byte, the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed); the flat-EQ identity law (unity on both bands the identity on both channels, the bands reconstructing the input, and bass moving direct current while treble leaves it untouched, the high band zero at direct current so the knobs are orthogonal); the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the shelf, the shared knobs holding the image); and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law (an all-silent master shelving to silence on both, the lengths held, a zero or above-one coefficient, a zero bass or treble denominator, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-shelf: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each shelved through ALES41's own scratch-low-band pass, every band scale and sum run in i64 so nothing overflows before the single clamp per channel; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a shelf writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the coefficient, both band gains, and the span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never shelves one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoShelfError is ALES41's ShelfError (BadCoeff, BadGain, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo shelf law is proven by running both stereo_shelf and ALES41's mono shelf on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the flat-EQ identity law proves unity on both bands is the input byte for byte on both and that a settled constant is doubled by a bass gain and untouched by a treble gain on each channel; the image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical through the shelf; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the five refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the coefficient over sample indices rather than a crossover in hertz, the gains plain fractions rather than decibels, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_shelf"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_shelf.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-shelf: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_shelf.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-shelf: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-shelf: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-shelf' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-shelf: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-shelf line"
